Permission allow rule (../../../.claude/settings.json): Write(.claude/**) is not matched by file permission checks — only Edit(path) rules are. Use Edit(.claude/**) instead (Edit rules cover all file-editing tools).
Read `references/a11y.md`, `form.md`, `life.md`, `api.md`. Two rules drove the shape of this fix.

The root cause is that everything is keyed on `error`. A required-and-empty group has `validity === 'valueMissing'` with no consumer-supplied `error`, so `changedProperties.has('error')` never fires and nothing is exposed. Bind off authoritative validity, declaratively, on the element that carries the role (CS-A11Y-001).

```js
  /**
   * Single authoritative predicate for "is this group invalid".
   * @returns {Boolean}
   */
  get isInvalid() {
    return Boolean(this.validity && this.validity !== 'valid');
  }

  updated(changedProperties) {
    // Mirror invalidity onto each child. Keyed on validity, not `error`:
    // `valueMissing` arrives with no consumer message at all.
    if (changedProperties.has('error') || changedProperties.has('validity')) {
      this.items.forEach((el) => {
        if (this.isInvalid) el.setAttribute('error', '');
        else el.removeAttribute('error');
      });
    }
  }

  render() {
    return html`
      <fieldset
        role="radiogroup"
        aria-invalid="${this.isInvalid ? 'true' : nothing}"
        aria-describedby="${this.isInvalid ? 'radio-group-error' : nothing}">
        <legend><slot name="legend"></slot></legend>
        <slot></slot>
        ${this.isInvalid
          ? html`<p id="radio-group-error" role="alert">
              ${this.error || 'Please select an option.'}
            </p>`
          : nothing}
      </fieldset>
    `;
  }
}
```

What changed and why:

- **`aria-invalid` on the `<fieldset>`, not the host.** The `role="radiogroup"` lives in shadow DOM; an attribute set imperatively on the custom-element host would never be associated with it by assistive tech. `radiogroup` is on ARIA 1.2's supported-role list for `aria-invalid`, and the explicit override is correct here — `<fieldset>`'s implicit `group` is not a radiogroup (CS-A11Y-003).
- **`aria-invalid` rendered as `nothing` when valid**, so the attribute is absent rather than `"false"` — same signal, no stale attribute to reason about.
- **The message renders for any invalidity**, with a fallback for `valueMissing`. Previously a required-empty group rendered no `<p>` at all, so there was nothing to announce and nothing to describe. `aria-describedby` ties it to the group so it's read on focus, not only at the moment it appears.
- **Dropped `aria-live="assertive"`.** `role="alert"` already implies it; the pair is redundant and some combinations double-announce.
- **`updated()` now also fires on `validity`** so children get the error styling in the `valueMissing` case too.

Two follow-ups worth doing in the same pass: sibling components built from this template almost certainly carry the identical defect — check `auro-checkbox-group` for the same shape and copy this `isInvalid` predicate rather than re-expressing it (CS-A11Y-002). And a regression test should assert `aria-invalid="true"` on the `radiogroup` for a required-and-empty group with no `error` set — that's the case the original code silently missed.
